ctors into the matrix differential equation:\n\n- $a(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1-3t \\\\ 3t\\end{aligned}]$ $\\text{is}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}(1-3t)^{′} \\\\ (3t)^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1-3t \\\\ 3t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3(1-3t)+(-3)\\cdot 3t \\\\ 3(1-3t)+3\\cdot 3t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $b(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2(1-3t) \\\\ 6t\\end{aligned}]$ $\\text{is}$ also a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}(2(1-3t))^{′} \\\\ (6t)^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2(1-3t) \\\\ 6t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3\\cdot 2(1-3t)+(-3)\\cdot 6t \\\\ 3\\cdot 2(1-3t)+3\\cdot 6t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $c(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}]$ $\\text{isn't}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}(t)^{′} \\\\ (t)^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3t+(-3)t \\\\ 3t+3t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-6t \\\\ 6t\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, consider the set of solutions ${a,b}.$ Its Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(a,b) & =|\\begin{aligned}1-3t & 2(1-3t) \\\\ 3t & 6t\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(1-3t)\\cdot 6t-2(1-3t)\\cdot 3t \\\\  & =6t(1-3t)-6t(1-3t) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is zero for all $t\\in R.$ Thus, since $a$ and $b$ are both solutions of the same linear system, but their Wronsk